WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.880
Tony Stark is a boss. He's so great, that even&nbsp;
his biggest mistakes are actually kinda awesome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.480
So why is his showdown against&nbsp;
the fake Mandarin one of his most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.200
underrated moments? Keep watching to find out!

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.360
During the final battle between&nbsp;
Tony Stark, the Iron Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.880
and Obadiah Stane, the Iron Monger, Stane's&nbsp;
suit is larger and seemingly more powerful.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.080
But when the combatants take to&nbsp;
the skies, it becomes clear that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.320
Stark has thought of something that&nbsp;
Obadiah didn't: the "icing problem."

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:35.600
Earlier in 2008's "Iron Man," Stark tests&nbsp;
out the flight systems of the Mark 2 suit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:40.640
for the first time and discovers that flying too&nbsp;
high causes the armor to freeze over and shut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.640
down. He promptly fixes this for the Mark 3 and&nbsp;
uses that to his advantage against Iron Monger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:51.840
but this moment is indicative of one of&nbsp;
the best through-lines in the entire MCU.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.040
The Iron Man armor doesn't just change over&nbsp;
time: Tony Stark adapts it from movie to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.920
movie based on the specific events of the&nbsp;
previous film. In "Iron Man 2," his suit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.560
struggles against Vanko's electrical whips,&nbsp;
but by the time of "Avengers," his armor can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:12.240
absorb excess electricity like it does when hit&nbsp;
by Thor's lightning. In "Infinity War," Stark's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.120
incredible nanotech armor is destroyed by Thanos&nbsp;
because, in part, the collapse of the suit's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.960
physical shields uses up the armor's limited&nbsp;
store of nanoparticles. By "Endgame," however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:28.000
Tony has implemented hard-light-style energy&nbsp;
shields to keep that from happening again.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.400
Seeing Tony's armor adapt to the specific&nbsp;
challenges within each film's narrative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:38.240
created an incredibly satisfying technological&nbsp;
evolutionary process, and that all started here.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.640
At the end of 2008's "Iron Man," Tony tells&nbsp;
the world that he's the pilot and inventor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.160
of the revolutionary new "Iron Man" armor, and&nbsp;
by the time its sequel rolls around in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:55.280
financial and political powers the world over&nbsp;
want that tech for themselves. To that end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.560
United States Senator Stern of&nbsp;
Pennsylvania leads hearings in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.880
which the man tries to force Stark to hand&nbsp;
over the armor to the federal government.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.080
"You want my property? You can't&nbsp;
have it! But I did you a big favor!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.120
I successfully privatized world peace!"

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.160
"F— you, Mr. Stark. F— you, buddy!"

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:17.760
By the end, however, after Iron Man and War&nbsp;
Machine defeat Whiplash and save the day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.920
the US decides to award the pair medals for their&nbsp;
service. Tony asks SHIELD Director Nick Fury to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:29.680
force Senator Stern to eat his words, present&nbsp;
the awards himself and personally congratulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:33.920
each of them. It's a great scene that's made&nbsp;
even richer after watching "Captain America:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:38.640
The Winter Soldier" and realizing that Stern&nbsp;
was a corrupt member of Hydra all along.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.017
"Hail Hydra!"

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.280
2012's "The Avengers" has one of the most&nbsp;
fondly remembered after-credits scenes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.320
the entire Marvel Cinematic Universe. After&nbsp;
delivering a nuclear missile through a portal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.200
and being caught and revived by the Hulk, Tony&nbsp;
asks the team if they want to get some grub.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.240
"You ever tried shawarma?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.040
There's a shawarma joint two blocks from here.&nbsp;
I don't know what it is, but I want to try it."

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:08.800
After the credits roll, audiences are treated&nbsp;
to the Avengers eating at that very restaurant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:13.360
Not only is it an unexpected payoff to a&nbsp;
seemingly throwaway line, but it's a great scene.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.000
What many fans may not realize, however, is&nbsp;
that the post-credits scene was set up earlier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.040
After flying inside of a Chitauri leviathan&nbsp;
Jonah-style and blowing it up from the inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:27.680
he crashes to the ground right next to the&nbsp;
shawarma joint before rejoining the fight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.160
It's an incredibly easy-to-miss detail and&nbsp;
one of the best Easter eggs in the film.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:37.360
It may not have been what fans expected,&nbsp;
but when "Iron Man 3" depicted Tony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.720
Stark struggling with symptoms of&nbsp;
post-traumatic stress disorder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.920
Iron Man became one of the most grounded&nbsp;
and human characters in the entire MCU.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:49.840
After nearly dying multiple times in a&nbsp;
battle for the fate of the entire planet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.480
Tony doesn't just walk away. By having Tony&nbsp;
deal with these very human mental health&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:59.200
consequences of the trauma he experienced,&nbsp;
Iron Man simultaneously became both a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:04.240
character and a representative of everybody&nbsp;
in the real world confronting these issues.

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:09.280
As clinical psychologist and Marvel&nbsp;
fan Dr. Andrea Letamendi wrote,

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.000
"Tony's struggle with anxiety is poignant&nbsp;
because it allows us to realize that he is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:17.840
in fact, still human. To this end,&nbsp;
it doesn't matter to me if his panic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:24.000
attacks are indicative of clinical PTSD,&nbsp;
complex PTSD, subclinical anxiety disorder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.240
or another psychiatric category we&nbsp;
can use as a label. The point is this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.880
A brilliant, powerful, and tough guy can&nbsp;
be vulnerable, scared, and confused."

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:39.520
"Iron Man 3" devotes most of its run time&nbsp;
to showing that it's Tony Stark's character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.880
not his armor, that makes&nbsp;
him a hero. He's Iron Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.320
regardless of what tools or&nbsp;
weapons he has at his disposal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:51.760
and one extended action sequence in particular&nbsp;
demonstrates that idea in action beautifully.

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.720
Cut off from his friends and normal equipment,&nbsp;
Stark visits a generic retail store and creates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.600
new weapons out of the everyday items he can&nbsp;
find there. Armed with only this, his training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.600
and his wits, he successfully breaches&nbsp;
the defenses of the mansion allegedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:09.520
belonging to the Mandarin, taking out&nbsp;
its guards with his jury-rigged gear.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.440
Just like when he finds himself imprisoned&nbsp;
by the Ten Rings in the original "Iron Man,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:18.880
Tony Stark becomes Iron Man by creating the best&nbsp;
tools he has out of the technology around him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:23.840
Though these results may not be as flashy as&nbsp;
his classic armor, they are just as meaningful.

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:28.640
When Tony Stark has JARVIS enact the&nbsp;
"Clean Slate Protocol" and destroy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:32.720
his entire armory of suits at the&nbsp;
end of "Iron Man 3," many viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:36.880
were not happy. It's a controversial&nbsp;
moment in an equally divisive film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:41.360
but it manages to perfectly capture the movie's&nbsp;
themes in a way that is often overlooked.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:45.600
The majority of the film revolves around&nbsp;
showing, not telling, that Tony Stark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.640
is Iron Man, not his armor, and this is the&nbsp;
scene that drives that home. Tony was coping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:55.760
with the trauma he experienced in New York, in&nbsp;
part, by overly-relying on his suits of armor.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:00.800
Those armors represented that unhealthy and&nbsp;
destructive obsession, and obliterating them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:05.840
was a determined symbol of his recommitment to&nbsp;
being present in both his own and Pepper's lives.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:12.800
By the time 2016's "Captain America: Civil&nbsp;
War" split the Avengers apart, Tony Stark and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:18.480
Pepper Potts were taking a break. When Steve asks&nbsp;
about the couple during the film, Tony explains,

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.880
"A few years ago I almost lost her, so I trashed&nbsp;
all my suits. Then, we had to mop up Hydra,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.880 --> 00:06:30.080
and then Ultron. My fault. And then,&nbsp;
and then, and then, I never stopped."

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.480
The line is an incredible piece of dialogue&nbsp;
that easily and concisely tracks Tony's personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:41.200
journey from "Iron Man 3" to "Civil War," and&nbsp;
there are a lot of interesting details that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:46.240
can be inferred from this. For example, if this&nbsp;
timeline is taken literally, Tony didn't make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:51.120
another Iron Man suit until after the events&nbsp;
of "Captain America: The Winter Soldier."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.760
This would explain where he was during the events&nbsp;
of the film and could mean that the Helicarriers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:00.720
crashing into Washington, DC's Potomac River&nbsp;
are what spurred him to put the armor back on.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.000
Additionally, "Civil War" shows that putting&nbsp;
the armor on again in "Avengers: Age of Ultron"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.840
impacted his relationship with Pepper by damaging&nbsp;
the gesture he made with the Clean Slate Protocol.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:17.280
Tony's talk with Steve is an excellent recap of&nbsp;
how Tony got to where he is in this movie, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:22.240
it's the perfect set-up for the exploration of his&nbsp;
motivations that follow immediately afterwards.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.120
Fans around the world were thrilled&nbsp;
when Sony and Disney reached a deal that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:32.240
finally allowed Spider-Man to join the Marvel&nbsp;
Cinematic Universe, and the wallcrawler's films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:37.840
have been wildly successful. That being said,&nbsp;
there is one aspect of the adaptation that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:43.920
proven mildly divisive: Peter's almost&nbsp;
"sidekick" relationship to Tony Stark.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:49.520
Critics bemoan the idea that a character who may&nbsp;
just be the most popular and successful superhero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:55.200
in history was made into "Iron Man Jr." The&nbsp;
end of "Spider-Man: Homecoming," however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:00.160
is one moment that avoids that idea while&nbsp;
still utilizing their mentor relationship in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:06.000
a way that doesn't diminish either character but&nbsp;
instead builds them both up as their own heroes.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.400
Having finally beaten the Vulture, Tony&nbsp;
is ready to introduce Spider-Man to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.960
world as the newest member of the&nbsp;
Avengers — but Peter says, "No."

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:18.800
"I'd rather just stay on the ground for a&nbsp;
little while. Friendly, neighborhood Spider-Man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.520
Somebody's got to look out&nbsp;
for the little guy, right?"

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:27.360
Tony is impressed by Peter's maturity.&nbsp;
But he also assumed Peter would say yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:31.200
so he had already announced, organized,&nbsp;
and started the press conference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:35.200
and now the reason for it just left to&nbsp;
go back to Queens. It's a great moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:40.240
that highlights Peter's development while also&nbsp;
generating some great humor at Tony's expense.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:41.360
"What am I going to tell them?"

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:47.280
"Think of something. How about… Hap, you&nbsp;
still have the ring? The engagement ring?"

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:50.160
"Are you kidding me? I've been&nbsp;
carrying this since 2008!"

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.120
After stowing away aboard a ship and sucking&nbsp;
Ebony Maw out into the vacuum of space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.120 --> 00:09:01.360
Tony Stark and Dr. Strange debate what to do&nbsp;
next. During their argument, Tony tells Strange

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:04.160
"Thanos has been inside my head for six years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:06.480
since he sent an army to New&nbsp;
York. And now he's back!"

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:13.440
That one sentence illustrates a key throughline of&nbsp;
Tony's journey since the original "Avengers" film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:16.240
After seeing what was out there&nbsp;
during the Battle for New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:19.200
Tony became haunted by what&nbsp;
else could be out there.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.600
It was that fear that drove him to dream&nbsp;
of the "suit of armor around the world"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:29.280
that unintentionally resulted in the creation of&nbsp;
Ultron and Vision in "Avengers: Age of Ultron."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.000
It was this dread that led to his decision&nbsp;
in "Captain America: Civil War" to choose the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:39.360
security of keeping the Avengers together over&nbsp;
the freedom of being able to act as they chose.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:43.920
After New York, Tony knew three things:&nbsp;
The aliens had come from somewhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:49.120
somebody had sent them, and both were still out&nbsp;
there, fully capable of returning. This knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.120 --> 00:09:54.640
influenced Tony's actions in every single film,&nbsp;
and that's encapsulated perfectly by this moment.

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:01.920
When they first met in 2012's "The Avengers,"&nbsp;
Tony and Steve didn't like each other one bit, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:06.720
after fighting for humanity together side-by-side,&nbsp;
they developed a strong friendship that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:11.600
ultimately shattered by the events of "Captain&nbsp;
America: Civil War." Unwilling to compromise and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:16.400
unable to talk things out, the two nearly killed&nbsp;
one another before going their separate ways.

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:22.240
After Thanos' victory at the end of "Infinity&nbsp;
War," that rift seemed wider than ever as Tony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:26.640
seemed to blame Steve for their loss.&nbsp;
However, once an opportunity to restore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:32.000
everything they'd lost finally appeared, the two&nbsp;
put their differences aside and worked together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:37.840
to gather the Infinity Stones from across time&nbsp;
and undo the snap that killed half the universe.

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:43.360
That entire personal journey comes to a head in a&nbsp;
singular moment from "Avengers: Endgame." Unable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:48.240
to retrieve the Tesseract and lacking the Pym&nbsp;
particles to try again and still make it home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:54.400
the pair's quest seems over until Tony realizes&nbsp;
they can find both in the 1970s during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.400 --> 00:11:00.480
early days of SHIELD at Camp Lehigh. If Stark's&nbsp;
wrong, everybody loses, and they'll become lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:06.640
in time. With literally half the universe riding&nbsp;
on this one chance, Tony looks at Steve and asks,

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:07.840
"You trust me?"

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:09.780
"I do."

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:15.840
With that, the pair's friendship is finally,&nbsp;
fully restored. The rift that was created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.640
during "Civil War" is mended, and they are&nbsp;
finally facing the end of the world united.

